643		\emph{generalized Pauli observables over $\F_q$} are the unitaries on
644		$\C^{\F_q}$
645		\[
646			\tau^X(a) = \sum_{j \in \F_q} \ket{j + a}\bra{j}
647			\qquad \text{and} \qquad
648			\tau^Z(b) = \sum_{j \in \F_q} \omega^{\tr(bj)} \ket{j}\bra{j}
649		\]
650		for $a, b \in \F_q$, where $\tr$ here denotes $\tr_{q \to p}$
651		(Definition~\ref{def:subfield-trace}); for admissible $q$ this agrees with
652		the standing shorthand, $p = 2$, and $\omega = -1$, so that all phases are
653		signs. All the $\tau^X$ operators commute with one another, as do all the
654		$\tau^Z$ operators. Their common eigenbases consist of the vectors
655		$\ket{e_W}$, labeled by $e \in \F_q$ and $W \in \{X, Z\}$, given by
656		\[
657			\ket{e_X} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{q}} \sum_{j \in \F_q}
658			\omega^{-\tr(ej)} \ket{j}\;, \qquad
659			\ket{e_Z} = \ket{e}\;;
660		\]
661		the Fourier transform
662		\begin{equation}\label{eq:fourier-f}
663			F = \frac{1}{\sqrt{q}} \sum_{a, b \in \F_q}
664			\omega^{-\tr(ab)} \ket{a}\bra{b}
665		\end{equation}
666		maps the computational ($Z$) eigenbasis to the $X$ eigenbasis. We write
667		$\{\tau^W_e\}_{e \in \F_q}$ for the projective measurement whose elements
668		are the rank-one projectors onto the vectors of the $W$ eigenbasis. For
669		systems of $n$ qudits, we write, for $W \in \{X,Z\}$ and $a \in \F_q^n$,
